+\begin{proof}
+Put $Q=\gen{\rho_j:j\in F}$ and
+$Z_j=O^{G_{\le j}}$ for $k-1\le j\le a-2$.
+The two natural $K$-rows of $O$ are also natural $Q$-orbits of
+size $q$. Each prefix $G_{\le j}$ in this range centralizes $Q$,
+so $Z_j$ is a union of $q$-point $Q$-orbits. It excludes the
+nonempty $Q$-fixed set $C$, hence is a proper subset of $\Omega$.
+
+For $k-1\le j\le a-3$, any mark of index at least $j+2$ outside
+$F$ fixes $O$: its index is at least $k+1$, so it lies outside
+$F\cup\{k\}$. It commutes with $G_{\le j}$ and therefore fixes
+$Z_j$ pointwise. The marks in $F$ preserve $Z_j$, and so does
+the prefix $G_{\le j}$. Consequently $\rho_{j+1}$ cannot preserve
+$Z_j$, or every generator of $G$ would preserve this proper set.
+Because $j+1\le a-2$, it commutes with $Q$ and any new point
+brings a whole additional $q$-point $Q$-orbit. Thus
+$|Z_{j+1}|\ge|Z_j|+q$.
+
+Lemma~\ref{rows} starts this chain with $|Z_{k-1}|\ge3q$.
+There are $a-k-1$ steps, including none when $a=k+1$.
+Hence $|Z_{a-2}|\ge(a-k+2)q$. Every mark after $x$ fixes $O$
+and commutes with $G_{\le a-2}$, so fixes $Z_{a-2}$ pointwise.
+This set is disjoint from the $K$-fixed set $C$, which the later
+tail also fixes. This proves (2).
+\end{proof}
